Bootstrapping

In this context, bootstrapping means escaping from a circular build dependency:
building gcc requires the runtime, but building the runtime requires gcc.

The solution is to fake things to enable us to build just enough gcc to build
the runtime


Build order when boostrapping:

0. cygwin-filesystem (bootstrap=1)

1. cygwin-binutils

2. cygwin-filesystem (bootstrap=0)
   (this package now acquires a dependency on cygwin-binutils-generic so its
   macros can strip binaries etc.)

3. cygwin-w32api-headers
   (this just installs files)

&  cygwin-bootstrap-headers
   (this just installs files)

   this provides the mininmal runtime headers needed by <windows.h>, so we can
   compile minimal gcc and then w32api-runtime

   it also provides a stub <new> header which is need to compile cygwin

4. cygwin-gcc (bootstrap=1)

5. cygwin-w32api-runtime

&  cygwin-default-manifest
   (bootstrap mode of this package seems questionable: it only needs
   target-windres and a cpp (but maybe the target-cpp)) (this package is needed
   to for building executables with the target compiler)

6. cygwin
   (this package contains the runtime library and headers, without any of the
   auxiliary programs or utilities etc., which aren't needed for
   cross-compiling)

7. cygwin-gcc (bootstrap=0)


now you have a working cross toolchain, you can build a target version of libbfd
(and it's dependencies: getttext, libiconv and zlib)

then you can cross-build a full cygwin package, with all utils etc.
